121317">PACE</span></strong><span style="color:#121317"> is fast. The expectations are high. The contribution matters. Working at TYTAN is more than a job, this is a mission. We push boundaries, think big, and hold ourselves to the highest standard. Our mission and values guide everything we do, and we're looking for people who are driven by ownership, embrace challenges, and are energized by meaningful work. If you're motivated by impact and thrive in dynamic environments, you'll fit right in.</span></p><p style="text-align:start;min-height: 1.7em;"></p><p style="text-align:start;"><span style="color:#121317">At </span><strong><span style="color:#121317">TYTAN</span></strong><span style="color:#121317">, we aim to set a global standard in autonomous air defense with AI-driven, cost-efficient systems that counter unmanned aerial threats at scale. We protect people. We protect critical infrastructure. We protect European sovereignty.</span></p><p style="min-height: 1.7em;"></p><p><span style="color:#121317">We are seeking a </span><strong><span style="color:#121317">Senior Software Engineer, Hardware-in-the-Loop Systems</span></strong><span style="color:#121317"> to join our Platform Team. Our interceptors fly autonomous missions from launch to intercept. Before a software change flies in the air, we want to see it fly on the bench: the real mission computer and flight controller, flying a full simulated mission in real time against simulated physics, sensors and targets. We are taking our hardware-in-the-loop testing to full closed-loop missions, and you lead that build.</span></p><p><span style="color:#121317">You are a builder, not a test author. You design the rigs, couple the simulation to the hardware, and build the framework so that our autonomy and perception engineers write and run their own HIL scenarios on every change.</span></p><p style="min-height: 1.7em;"></p><h3>Your roadmap to make an impact</h3><ul><li><p><strong><span style="color:#121317">The closed loop:</span></strong><span style="color:#121317"> Couple real-time physics, sensor and target simulation to real flight hardware. Inject IMU, GNSS, camera and other sensor data at the interface the hardware expects, close the loop on actuator commands, and keep latency, jitter and time synchronisation within budget, so a flight on the rig behaves like a flight in the air.</span></p></li><li><p><strong><span style="color:#121317">Fault injection:</span></strong><span style="color:#121317"> Sensor failures, GNSS denial and spoofing, link loss, bus errors and power faults, reproducible on demand.</span></p></li><li><p><strong><span style="color:#121317">System rigs:</span></strong><span style="color:#121317"> Rigs for the current and next generation mission computer and for full system configurations with ground station, radio link and interceptors. Provisioned as code, with automated deployment of builds, remote power control, health monitoring and self-recovery.</span></p></li><li><p><strong><span style="color:#121317">HIL in CI:</span></strong><span style="color:#121317"> Merge-gating regression suites, nightly and long-running mission suites, rig scheduling across pipelines, flaky test detection, and structured results traceable to requirements.</span></p></li><li><p><strong><span style="color:#121317">Test framework:</span></strong><span style="color:#121317"> Fixtures for devices, instruments and simulators, so that a domain engineer writes a HIL test as easily as a unit test, and the same scenario runs in SIL and HIL.</span></p></li><li><p><strong><span style="color:#121317">HIL as a product:</span></strong><span style="color:#121317"> Your users are our autonomy, perception and systems engineers. Availability and time to result are your metrics, also in restricted and air-gapped lab networks.</span></p></li></ul> <br> <ul><li><p><span style="color:#121317">4+ years building HIL or hardware test systems for embedded or autonomous systems, including at least one closed-loop rig you designed and built yourself, not only operated or wrote test cases for</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">Real-time simulation coupled to hardware: plant and sensor models running in real time against real hardware, on commercial HIL platforms or custom Linux-based rigs</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">Expert Python, plus C or C++ for real-time components</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">Strong Linux and CI: self-hosted runners with hardware in the loop, containerised test environments, device management, remote power and console control</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">Debugging across the hardware and software boundary: embedded interfaces such as CAN, UART, SPI and Ethernet, and timing problems you have measured, not guessed</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">Degree in Electrical Engineering, Aerospace Engineering, Computer Science or a related field, or equivalent experience</span></p></li></ul><p style="min-height: 1.7em;"></p><h3>Nice to haves</h3><ul><li><p><span style="color:#121317">Flight or vehicle dynamics models, and PX4 or ArduPilot SITL/HITL</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">HIL platforms such as dSPACE, NI PXI/VeriStand or Speedgoat</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">Sensor stimulation at the hardware interface: IMU, GNSS, camera and video injection</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">Time synchronisation (e.g. PTP), real-time Linux and jitter measurement</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">Test frameworks at scale (pytest or Robot Framework) and instrument control via SCPI or VISA</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">FMI/FMU or Gazebo-based simulation</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">MAVLink and flight controller interfaces</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color:#121317">GNSS simulators (e.g. Spirent, Safran Skydel) and RF environment simulation</span></p></